Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: mt7623: bananapi-r2: use the RGMII CPU port only

The BPI-R2 MT7530 switch declares two CPU ports: port6 (gmac0, TRGMII) and
port5 (gmac1, RGMII). gmac0's TRGMII RX clock does not reliably lock on this
board: after a cold boot the switch links up but the frame engine receives no
frames from the CPU port (gmac0 rx stays at 0), so all DSA user ports link at
1G but carry no traffic. The failure is a marginal-timing race - it hits
roughly 60% of cold boots - and no software reset recovers it deterministically
(a full mtk_soc_eth unbind/rebind only helps ~40% of the time). gmac1's plain
RGMII path, by contrast, is reliable.

Drop port6 (the TRGMII CPU port) and disable the now-unused gmac0 so gmac1/RGMII
(port5) is the sole CPU port / DSA conduit. This trades the TRGMII CPU port's
higher peak bandwidth for a reliable 1G RGMII path - the correct trade since the
TRGMII path does not work.

Verified on a Banana Pi R2 over 20+ cold power cycles: with the stock DTS the
switch data path is dead on the majority of cold boots; with this change wan/lan
come up and forward (DHCP + gateway + internet, 0% loss) on every cold boot with
no intervention, and no phantom gmac0 interface remains.

Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7623n-bananapi-bpi-r2.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7623n-bananapi-bpi-r2.dts
@@ -172,7 +172,7 @@
 };
 
 &gmac0 {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 	phy-mode = "trgmii";
 
 	fixed-link {
@@ -244,19 +244,6 @@
 
 					fixed-link {
 						speed = <1000>;
-						full-duplex;
-						pause;
-					};
-				};
-
-				port@6 {
-					reg = <6>;
-					label = "cpu";
-					ethernet = <&gmac0>;
-					phy-mode = "trgmii";
-
-					fixed-link {
-						speed = <1000>;
 						full-duplex;
 						pause;
 					};
